But the journey we have is different: they want Escape to close the dialog (modal) when focus is on the access button (not inside dropdown?). Actually step sequence: open share modal, press Enter (opens modal), then seek #access-toggle (focus on button), then press Escape. They expect the access-toggle to become invisible (value false). That suggests they think Escape should close the modal, making the button disappear (since modal hidden). However the observed after shows button still visible and aria-expanded false (meaning dropdown closed but modal still open). So Escape only closed dropdown, not modal.

Thus the bug: Escape key handling in modal should close modal when keyboard is enabled (modal.config.keyboard true). In modal.js, _setEscapeEvent adds keydown listener for Escape that calls hide() if keyboard true. However note that the modal also has a dropdown inside; the dropdown also listens for Escape via dataApiKeydownHandler (in dropdown.js) which calls clearMenus and focuses the button. The modal's Escape listener may be prevented because dropdown's keydown handler prevents default and stops propagation? Let's check.

In dropdown.js, dataApiKeydownHandler: if event.key === ESCAPE_KEY, then button.focus(); Dropdown.clearMenus(); return; It does event.preventDefault() and event.stopPropagation()? Actually at start: event.preventDefault(); event.stopPropagation(); Yes they call preventDefault and stopPropagation. So when Escape is pressed while focus is on the dropdown toggle (or inside dropdown menu), the dropdown's keydown handler runs, prevents default and stops propagation, thus modal's keydown listener never sees the event. Therefore modal does not close.

Goal: Escape should close the modal when focus is on the access toggle button (which is the dropdown toggle). According to regression guard, Escape inside the open access menu should close only the menu (i.e., dropdown). But here they want Escape to close the dialog when focus is on the access button (not inside menu). However the dropdown's keydown handler treats Escape same regardless of whether menu is open or not? Let's see: In dataApiKeydownHandler, condition: if (!isActive && (event.key === ARROW_UP_KEY || event.key === ARROW_DOWN_KEY)) { ... } else if (!isActive || event.key === SPACE_KEY) { Dropdown.clearMenus(); return; } For Escape, they have earlier block: if (event.key === ESCAPE_KEY) { const button = this.matches(SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) ? this : SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE)[0]; button.focus(); Dropdown.clearMenus(); return; }. This runs regardless of isActive? Actually they didn't check isActive for Escape. So Escape always triggers clearing menus and focusing button, even if menu not active. That means when dropdown is not shown (menu closed), pressing Escape will still call clearMenus (which does nothing if not shown) and focus button. It also prevents default and stopPropagation, thus modal's Escape is swallowed.

Thus to allow Escape to close modal when dropdown menu is not shown, we need to modify dropdown's keydown handler to not preventDefault/stopPropagation when menu is not shown? Or we need to let Escape propagate to modal when menu is not shown. The regression guard expects that when menu is open, Escape closes only menu (not modal). So we need to differentiate: if dropdown menu is shown (isActive true), then Escape should close menu only and not propagate; if menu is not shown, Escape should propagate to allow modal to close.

